{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-103", "type": "attests", "to": "person:ananias-son-of-nedebaeus", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.103 names the same man: Herod of Chalcis 'made Ananias, the son of Nebedeu' high priest (c. 47/48 CE) - the same person and office Acts 23:2 shows."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-103", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-02-ananias-strikes-paul", "grade": "B", "note": "Ant. 20.103 places Ananias son of Nedebaeus in the high priesthood during Cumanus's procuratorship (c. 47/48 CE onward), covering Paul's hearing before the council c. 57-59 - it attests the setting (the office-holder and his tenure), not the command to strike, which is the narrative's own report. Regraded A to B by the architect; the office-grade A anchors claim:ac-23-02-ananias-high-priest."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-205-206", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-01-03-strike-and-protest", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 20.205-206 shows the same Ananias a 'great hoarder up of money' whose wicked servants took tithes 'by violence' and beat those who resisted - the recorded violence of the man, on a later occasion (under Albinus, 62-63 CE), not this hearing."}
{"from": "site:antonia-fortress", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-10-rescue", "grade": "B", "note": "The Herodian citadel adjoining the Temple was the tribune's headquarters; its barracks are the place Paul was again taken into."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-238-247", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-10-rescue", "grade": "B", "note": "War 5.5.8: the tower of Antonia housed 'the guards' - the garrison whose soldiers the tribune sent down to rescue Paul from the council."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-08-sadducees-no-resurrection", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.1.4: 'the doctrine of the Sadducees is this: That souls die with the bodies' - the same group and the same doctrine in substance."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-164-165", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-08-sadducees-no-resurrection", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, War 2.8.14: the Sadducees 'take away the belief of the immortal duration of the soul, and the punishments and rewards in Hades' - the same denial of life after death."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-08-pharisees-resurrection", "grade": "A", "note": "Ant. 18.1.3: the Pharisees 'believe that souls have an immortal rigor in them', receive rewards or punishments under the earth, and 'shall have power to revive and live again' - the same resurrection doctrine Acts 23:8 reports."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-nedarim-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-12-14-vow", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Nedarim 1:1: a vow renders an object forbidden 'as though it were a sacrificial offering', and binds a thing 'as though it were a sacrificial offering' - the same institution of self-imposed abstention vows; redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the 50s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:cassius-dio-60-17-5-6", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-26-lysias-name", "grade": "C", "note": "Dio 60.17.7 (same fetched page): information was laid against new citizens 'to the effect that they were not adopting Claudius' name' - new citizens under Claudius took the emperor's nomen; the tribune's name Claudius Lysias fits a man enfranchised under Claudius (brief lead C)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-137-144", "type": "attests", "to": "person:antonius-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.137: 'Claudius sent Felix, the brother of Pallas, to take care of the affairs of Judea' - the same man Acts 23:24 calls 'Felix the governor'."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-137-144", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-24-30-letter", "grade": "B", "note": "Ant. 20.137-144 confirms Felix held the procuratorship of Judea (from Claudius's twelfth year, 52/53 CE), the office the letter addresses; the letter itself has no external witness. Regraded A to B by the architect: Josephus is A for Felix and his office (person:antonius-felix), but the claim is the tribune's letter, which has no external witness. Split per the architect's titles/offices ruling: the governor-grade A now anchors claim:ac-23-26-felix-governor; this edge keeps B for the letter event."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-5-9", "type": "attests", "to": "person:antonius-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Tacitus, Hist. 5.9 names Antonius Felix among those to whom Claudius 'entrusted the province of Judæa' - an independent second naming of the same procurator."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-12-54", "type": "attests", "to": "person:antonius-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Tacitus, Ann. 12.54: Felix 'had for some time been governor of Judaea' - independent confirmation of the man and his office."}
{"from": "site:caesarea-promontory-palace", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-35-herods-palace", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated promontory palace is Herod's own building at Caesarea ('Herod built his palace on a promontory jutting out into the sea, with a decorative pool surrounded by stoas') - the Herodian governor's residence Acts 23:35 calls 'Herod's palace'; grade B for the building and setting, not the event of Paul's lodging."}
{"from": "observation:antipatris-leg-distances", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-23-31-antipatris", "grade": "C", "note": "The two legs are computed from coordinates at about 46 km (Jerusalem to Antipatris) and 44 km (Antipatris to Caesarea) - a demanding but feasible night march of about seven hours for infantry followed by a next-day cavalry ride; geography of the route, not the event."}
